{
 "id": "2022-kawshalya-visual",
 "name": "Visual Complexity, Safety and Preference of Streets",
 "description": "Is a busier street scene safer or scarier? Kawshalya, Weerasinghe & Chandrasekara (2022, PLOS ONE) took 48 Google Street View images within 1 km of Colpetty Junction in Colombo, Sri Lanka, and measured their visual complexity in two ways: a Shannon Diversity Index (SDI) of ten scene components (building façade, vegetation, sidewalk and street furniture, road, advertisements, sky, vehicles, pedestrians, open space, water) and a box-counting fractal dimension. 78 respondents from design-related fields rated every image on a 1–7 scale for perceived safety, preference (comfort) and perceived complexity, imagining they stood at the spot looking along the street. Safety and preference followed an inverted U with SDI: medium-complexity streets were seen as safest and most preferred, and both very simple and very complex streets scored low. SDI and fractal dimension both correlated with perceived complexity. Analysis as in the paper: group images into low / medium / high levels of each component (present / absent for advertisements, pedestrians, open space and water), then Kruskal–Wallis and Mann–Whitney tests, plus the SDI–rating curve. The paper's data are open (S1 Data, XLSX): every respondent's 144 ratings and every image's component percentages, keyed SVI_1 … SVI_48. This template keys its images the same way (svi_01.jpg = SVI_1), so new ratings can be set beside the original ones image by image. This is a method replication: the paper did not publish its images. Differences from the original study: (1) the images are new Google Street View captures (2022, © Google) taken at the 40 points we could read from the paper's map (Fig 1); SVI_03 and SVI_04 share one map point and are shown as the forward and backward view; SVI_05, 10, 14, 27, 31, 32, 41 and 43 are missing (not on the map, or no Street View nearby); the viewing direction is the direction of travel, so views differ from the originals and the paper's component percentages do not apply to these images; (2) the anchors of the 1–7 scales are our wording; the paper gives none; (3) the image order is randomised per participant; the paper does not report its order; (4) the paper recruited design professionals and students by snowball sampling; the template adds an optional question on design background instead of restricting the sample; (5) the answer options for age, residence and street use follow the paper's data file, and the gender question adds \"Other / prefer not to say\".",
